19. PERFORMANCE AND RESOURCE MANAGEMENT

Performance is a first-class requirement.

The application should feel responsive even on ordinary student laptops.

Rendering

- Use retained drawing data.
- Avoid full-surface redraws.
- Use viewport culling.
- Use spatial indexing.
- Use efficient invalidation.
- Avoid excessive Dispatcher calls.
- Reuse stroke buffers.
- Keep pointer processing separate from expensive recognition.

Recognition

- Run asynchronously.
- Process only changed regions.
- Cache results.
- Support cancellation.
- Avoid full-document OCR unnecessarily.
- Load ML models only when needed.

Memory

- Bound undo history.
- Lazy-load pages.
- Release unused image buffers.
- Cache thumbnails.
- Avoid duplicate image decoding.
- Dispose native resources.
- Monitor memory pressure.

CPU

- No busy loops.
- No continuous unnecessary polling.
- No background AI processing while idle.
- Use event-driven processing.
- Avoid unnecessary timers.

Target smooth 60 FPS or better when the hardware permits.

Measure actual performance instead of claiming arbitrary performance numbers.

